All tests pass (276 state, 119 tool_wrappers). Here's what changed:

### Changes made

**`src/state.rs` (panic hook, line 62-71):**
• Before building the FailureObserved payload, reads `CURRENT_TOOL_NAME` via `with(|c| c.take())` — the same pattern used by `CURRENT_MODEL_CALL_ID` below
• When the thread-local has a value, adds `"tool_name"` to the payload; otherwise the field is absent (panic outside tool execution)
• Uses `c.take()` so the name is consumed and can't leak between panics

**`src/tool_wrappers.rs` (RecoveryHintTool::execute, lines 1192-1194):**
• Before `self.inner.execute()`: calls `crate::state::set_current_tool_name(tool_name.clone())` to record which tool is executing
• Immediately creates `let _guard = crate::state::CurrentToolNameGuard` — the guard's `Drop` calls `c.take()` on `CURRENT_TOOL_NAME`, ensuring cleanup on panic, early return, or cancellation

### How it works end-to-end
1. `RecoveryHintTool::execute` sets the thread-local with the tool name, then invokes the inner tool
2. If the inner tool panics, the panic hook fires on the same thread, reads the tool name from the thread-local, and includes it in the `FailureObserved` payload
3. The `CurrentToolNameGuard` Drop ensures the thread-local is cleared regardless of outcome
4. If a panic occurs outside any tool (e.g., in prompt handling), `CURRENT_TOOL_NAME` is `None` and `tool_name` is simply omitted from the payload
